  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several strategies for building tissue adhesives or hydrogel coatings by decorating polymer networks with complementary chemical groups and using different intermolecular interactions at a tissue surface. Across the panels, blue polymer chains carry reactive motifs that bind to tissue through orange surface groups, forming a conformal interfacial network.\n\nThe top row highlights covalent coupling approaches based on common reactive pairs, where polymers bearing amine-, aldehyde-, or related activated ester functionalities appear to anchor onto tissue through interfacial bond formation. These schemes emphasize how functionalized chains create adhesion by directly reacting with groups present on the tissue.\n\nThe central part shifts to a more multifunctional network, apparently involving catechol-containing units and metal coordination. It illustrates several noncovalent and dynamic interactions—hydrogen bonding, cation–π interaction, and Schiff-base formation—working together. A labeled example, rGO@PDA, suggests reduced graphene oxide coated with polydopamine embedded in the network. The lower key links green hexagons, blue stars, and red bursts to catechol-like groups, Fe-based coordination nodes, and another crosslinking motif.\n\nThe bottom panel depicts a polyelectrolyte-style system where amine and carboxyl groups contribute to adhesion through electrostatic interaction and chain entanglement or “physical interpenetration,” further stabilizing attachment to tissue.",
